1
00:00:03,401 --> 00:00:09,757
Deep dive: CAM end to end. A spice-rack side from a DXF, through its operations, their order, the tools they pick, and the simulation.

2
00:00:09,757 --> 00:00:20,947
Import drawing. The largest closed shape becomes the outline, and every CAD layer is kept, because the layers tell the app what to machine.

3
00:00:20,947 --> 00:00:31,112
It was drawn in millimetres: 14 pieces become 3 closed shapes, 1 open path and 10 circles. The part reads 9-13/16" x 17-11/16" in this inch shop.

4
00:00:31,112 --> 00:00:42,725
Machining rules turn layer names into operations: DRILL_12 is a 12 mm deep drill, POCKET_6 a 6 mm pocket. Agree layer names with your designer once, and imports need no clicks.

5
00:00:42,725 --> 00:00:52,365
INSIDE and OUTLINE cut through. NOTES has no rule, so it is listed and left alone: a dimension line never gets machined by mistake.

6
00:00:52,365 --> 00:01:01,380
Join ends closer than... closes small gaps CAD drawings often have. An open outline can’t be cut as a part.

7
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:12,723
Pick the material, so thickness and "through" depths are right, then Create part.

8
00:01:12,723 --> 00:01:24,544
Four operations, in program order: drill, pocket, inside cut, then the outline. The order is the point.

9
00:01:24,544 --> 00:01:33,348
Small work first, outline last: while the part is still joined to the sheet, the vacuum holds it firmly. Cut it free first and it can shift.

10
00:01:33,348 --> 00:01:47,154
Open the drill. Tool: Match each hole diameter. The 5 mm holes pick T201, a 5 mm drill, so a hole is never routed or drilled the wrong size.

11
00:01:47,154 --> 00:02:00,604
Open the pocket. It uses T101, the 12 mm compression cutter, 1/4" deep. Depth per pass 0 means the tool’s own step-down.

12
00:02:00,604 --> 00:02:11,273
Strategy: follow the shape, climb milling for a clean wall in melamine. Stepover 45% of the tool still has a Configure badge: your number, not ours.

13
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:21,330
Entry: a helix would be gentlest, but it doesn’t fit at this pocket’s start, so the app ramps in instead and says so.

14
00:02:21,330 --> 00:02:29,956
Rest machining and finish passes are there for tight corners and fine walls. Not needed on a 1/4" pocket with a 12 mm tool.

15
00:02:29,956 --> 00:02:39,473
Sort by tool would group work by tool to save changes; the cut-out still stays last. Here it is already one drill and one cutter.

16
00:02:39,473 --> 00:02:43,441
Checked them all? Accept all.

17
00:02:43,441 --> 00:03:02,285
Simulate plays the toolpaths in program order on a block of the real board thickness.

18
00:03:02,285 --> 00:03:12,333
Each operation with its time; the totals show cut length, material removed and the deepest cut, to catch a depth typo before it hits the spoilboard.

19
00:03:12,333 --> 00:03:32,572
3D shows the finished side: holes, pocket and handle cut-out. Drag to turn it.

20
00:03:32,572 --> 00:03:50,187
The collision check looks for the shank, the holder or a rapid move hitting material. It checks our toolpaths; simulate in woodWOP too.

21
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:55,468
So: name layers by rule, keep the cut-out last, let each hole pick its drill, configure your own feeds and stepover, and simulate.
